5:[["$","$L10","3.2",{"topic":{"id":"3.2","title":"OSINT Investigation Methodology","minutes":13,"summary":"Use a repeatable cycle of direction, collection, processing, analysis, dissemination, and review.","sections":[{"title":"Direction and controlled collection","body":["Translate the complaint into prioritized intelligence requirements. Identify known identifiers, time ranges, confidence levels, and gaps. Decide which public sources can answer each question and what authority would be needed for non-public records. Establish collection boundaries before curiosity expands the scope.","Capture the original page or record, URL, time, and relevant context. Use a controlled environment for untrusted content and avoid signing into a target account. Preserve a clean copy and record transformations. Where a platform limits automated collection, respect the applicable restrictions and choose a lawful alternative rather than bypassing controls."]},{"title":"Processing and analytical discipline","body":["Normalize names, dates, and identifiers without silently changing the source. Build an entity table for accounts, domains, organizations, and payment references. Represent a relationship as an edge with a source and confidence, not an unexplained line on a chart. Keep contradictory records instead of deleting them to make the graph look convincing.","Use analysis of competing hypotheses. For a shared alias, consider the same operator, imitation, shared marketing staff, or an old transferred account. Seek discriminating evidence: a distinctive payment reference, independently verified registry contact, or a verified account migration notice. Repetition across scraper sites may still originate from one unsupported claim."]},{"title":"Dissemination, review, and correction","body":["Produce a concise assessment with key judgments, supporting observations, source references, confidence, and alternatives. Mark inference explicitly. Share only with authorized recipients and remove unnecessary personal information from presentation copies. Preserve the underlying records under the retention policy.","Review the assessment when new evidence arrives or a source is corrected. Maintain an audit trail of changes and avoid silently rewriting history. An intelligence lead can justify further lawful collection, but it is not itself a conviction or a warrant. The handoff should state what investigators still need to establish."]}],"practice":{"title":"One alias, two explanations","scenario":"A public shop profile and a suspicious payment page share the alias “northstar-demo”.","steps":["Record both sources and the exact matching string.","Write same-operator and imitation hypotheses.","Seek an independent public identifier that discriminates between them.","Deliver a bounded judgment with confidence and next steps."],"debrief":"Matching text is a lead. Independent, contextual corroboration is what changes its investigative value."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss"],"sources":["nist-guide","privacy","first"],"labId":"osint","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain direction and controlled collection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain processing and analytical discipline and apply it to an investigation.","Explain dissemination, review, and correction and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.2","prompt":"Why use analysis of competing hypotheses?","options":["To make every clue look equally certain.","To reduce confirmation bias by testing plausible alternatives.","To avoid documenting sources.","To prove identity from a username alone."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Competing hypotheses help identify evidence that distinguishes explanations rather than merely supports a favorite one.","unit":3}}}]]
